body:
**[comments] Rule 4, comment lines that map to no code line: shorten**

The 17-line comment sits above two std::function declarations, and its lines 67-77 (the K VNNI line geometry, the V pair interleave, and why both are the AMX B-operand order) map to no line of gof.hpp: they restate Note [K VNNI storage] (kv_cache.hpp:653-661, 672-673), the k_vnni.hpp header (lines 13-17, 29-30) and Note [VNNI Packed V layout] (v_vnni.hpp:33-44). The contract facts that the types do not carry (row size and order, page-or-scratch aliasing of the returned pointer, scratch ownership and 64-byte alignment) stay as three lines plus one pointer line to the two Notes. Lines 59-60 are unchanged context and are repeated verbatim in the proposed text.

Proposed:
```cpp
  // Opaque pointers to the page's K/V accessors. Templated page
  // type is erased behind lambdas that populate() invokes.
  // k_head_fn(slot, kv_head, scratch) -> bf16* to this token's K row.
  // v_head_fn(slot, kv_head, scratch) -> bf16* to this token's V row.
  // A row is HW_BAKED_HEAD_SIZE bf16 values in dimension order. The pointer is
  // into the page when the page stores the row contiguously, else into the
  // caller-owned, 64-byte-aligned scratch where the callback rebuilt the row.
  // The row is spread for K under TRON_K_VNNI (Note [K VNNI storage],
  // kv_cache.hpp) and for V always (Note [VNNI Packed V layout], v_vnni.hpp).
```

**[comments] Rule 4, comment lines that map to no code line: shorten**

The sentence 'Until the VNNI AMX kernel is merged it serves every page of a VNNI slot; after that, partial pages, hosts without AMX and the AMX kill switch' is the dispatch story: who calls this reader and when. Note [K VNNI storage] (items 2 and 3 and its kill-switch paragraph) owns that story and the header already points there, so the copy here is cut and the AMX definition moves to the sentence that now mentions AMX first.

Proposed:
```cpp
// The AVX-512 (the CPU's 512-bit vector instruction set) QK score reader of the
// K VNNI layout (h/tron/tensor/k_vnni.hpp). No AMX (Advanced Matrix Extensions,
// the tile instruction set) instruction runs here. So any translation unit may
// include it. The layout, who writes it and who reads it are described in
// Note [K VNNI storage] (in kv_cache.hpp).
//
// Words used here: bf16 is the 16-bit brain floating-point format. fp16 is the
// 16-bit IEEE half format. fp32 is the 32-bit IEEE single format. VDPBF16PS is
// the AVX-512 instruction that multiplies bf16 pairs and adds them in fp32.
```

**[comments] Rule 4, comment lines that map to no code line: shorten**

Of 21 lines, these do not map to a code line or restate something already written: '(the caller keeps them at -inf)' is caller context (self_attention.hpp:1906); 'Token lanes ... no horizontal reduction and no per-token gather' repeats Note [K VNNI storage] item 3 and the intrinsic names at lines 96-104; 'k_page is the page's packed K plane ... allowed to take the plane address' restates the parameter type and the k_vnni_access comment in tensor/k_vnni.hpp; 'a group of more than 16 heads is scored 16 heads at a time' restates lines 65-70; the float bullet's widening repeats the in-body comment at 107-108; the fp16 bullet restates lines 72-76. The add-order fact of the split ('Every head's scores are its own accumulation. So the split changes no head's add order') moves next to the split at line 66 (see that finding).

Proposed:
```cpp
// Scores of one query group against the live tokens of one page, read from
// the VNNI plane with AVX-512. s[h * s_stride + t] = q_h . K_t (UNSCALED) for
// every token t whose bit is set in `live`. The other entries of s are left
// untouched. The K storage of a token whose bit is clear is never read (masked
// loads). So unwritten tokens cannot change the result. q points at kv_mul rows
// of 128 values (the group's heads, row stride 128). A bf16 q adds the same
// VDPBF16PS products as the row-major dotter<bf16, 128>, and a float q the same
// FMA (fused multiply-add) products as dotter<float, 128>. Only the order of the
// fp32 adds differs (Note [AMX attention dispatch]).
```

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

Inside Note [K VNNI storage], the last sentence of item 6 ('They take one token's row as a typed view (const_k_row_view / k_row_view)') restates the parameter types of set_k_row / get_k_row and the comment on those aliases in k_vnni.hpp. The rest of item 6 stays; the anchor is the item's lines, not the whole Note.

Proposed:
```cpp
//   6. the row view page::k() does not exist for VNNI slots. A token's values
//      are not contiguous there. The layout-agnostic set_k_row / get_k_row
//      accessors exist for every slot.
```

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

'k is the typed owner k_vnni_tensor' and '(TRON_K_VNNI, 128-dimension heads)' restate the std::conditional_t on k_vnni::layout_on, and 'Both hold page_size * head_size bf16 values' restates the static_assert two lines below ('packed K holds the same bytes as native K'). The last sentence explains what std::conditional_t does with the unselected type, a language fact (Rule 6); if a reviewer asks why k_vnni_tensor's static_assert does not fire for other head sizes, answer in the review.

Proposed:
```cpp
  // Native K: letting p index the token within a page and i index into
  // head_size, k is indexed as [p * head_size + i], one row of head_size bf16
  // values per token. Packed K: see Note [K VNNI storage].
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
lines 1-2 and 'values per token.' -> `using native_k_storage = bf16[page_size * head_size];`; 'Packed K: see Note [K VNNI storage].' -> `using k_storage = std::conditional_t<k_vnni::layout_on<head_size>, k_vnni_tensor<...>, native_k_storage>;`; the two static_asserts carry their own messages

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

Sentences two and three restate the #ifdef TRON_K_VNNI and the head_size == HEAD_SIZE_128 test below. The facts the code does not say are that TRON_K_VNNI comes from CMake and that the gate ignores the query type and AMX availability, which is the invariant the Note [K VNNI storage] also states.

Proposed:
```cpp
// The layout gate. TRON_K_VNNI is a CMake option. The gate depends on the head
// size only, not on the query type or on AMX availability. So every reader and
// writer of a slot sees the same layout.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
line 1 (CMake option) -> #ifdef TRON_K_VNNI; line 2 (head size only) -> layout_on = head_size == HEAD_SIZE_128, the only parameter; line 3 (same layout for every reader and writer) -> the two definitions take no other input

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

"One element is read through at(token, dim)" restates the at() method. "Views come only from an owner" restates the private constructor and the friend k_vnni_tensor. "A writable view converts to a read-only one, never the reverse" restates the requires clause of the converting constructor. The list of absent members (no pointer constructor, no data(), no operator[], no conversion) is visible in the struct body. The two facts that stay are the no-element-write rule and why there is no pointer access.

Proposed:
```cpp
// A packed K plane of [Rows tokens * Cols dimensions]. There is no element write:
// rows enter only through store_row and copy_token. There is no public pointer
// access: a bare pointer does not say which layout it points at.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
line 1 (the plane shape, no element write) -> template <typename T, size_t Rows, size_t Cols> and the single const accessor at(); line 2 (rows enter through store_row and copy_token) -> friend struct detail::k_vnni_access, the route those operations use; line 3 (no pointer access) -> private explicit k_vnni_view(T* data)

**[comments] Rule 4, comment lines that map to no code line: shorten**

"Exactly one 64-byte-aligned bf16 array, no separate allocation" restates alignas(64) bf16 data_[Rows * Cols]. "Trivial to construct, copy and destroy; copying an owner copies the plane" restates the defaulted special members and is enforced by the static_assert in kv_cache.hpp (line 2529). The page::copy_storage_slot parenthetical is caller context that already sits at the caller (kv_cache.hpp lines 2287-2291). "as_view() on a temporary is deleted" restates the two = delete lines, and the lifetime rule is carried by TRON(this_lifetimebound). What stays: why there is no constructor, and why the alignment is 64 bytes.

Proposed:
```cpp
// Owns one packed K plane. No initialization: the cache's DMA allocation creates
// the kv_blocks that hold it (Note [KV block lifetime] in kv_cache.hpp). 64-byte
// alignment: every pair row is one 64-byte line.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
line 1 (owns one plane, no initialization) -> bf16 data_[Rows * Cols] and the absence of a constructor; line 2 (who creates it, the Note) -> same absence of a constructor; line 3 (64-byte alignment) -> struct alignas(64) and alignas(64) bf16 data_

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

"Contiguous ... head_size bf16 values" restates const_view<bf16, ..., seq<Cols>, sseq<1>>, and "the caller's alignment and DMA flags travel with the row" restates the Aligned and Dma template parameters. "For page::set_k_row / page::get_k_row" is caller context; the aliases are also the parameter types of store_row and load_row in this file. The fact that stays is that the Aligned flag, which defaults to VIEW_ALIGNED_TRUE, is not a requirement.

Proposed:
```cpp
// Transfer rows: one token's values in dimension order, not packed storage.
// Aligned is not a precondition (unlike the packed V rows): store_row and load_row
// use unaligned loads and stores.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
line 1 (one token's values in order, not packed) -> seq<Cols>, sseq<1> of the alias; line 2 (Aligned is not a precondition) -> bool Aligned = VIEW_ALIGNED_TRUE; line 3 (unaligned loads and stores) -> same default, explained by _mm512_loadu_si512 / _mm512_storeu_si512 in store_row and load_row

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

The clause "in the K VNNI layout of h/tron/tensor/k_vnni.hpp" restates the name k_vnni and the file pointer the header already gives at lines 5-6, and the "Note [K VNNI storage] in kv_cache.hpp" pointer repeats line 8 of the same file. Dropping both leaves the block in the same shape as the existing Packed V block (lines 28-33), with the facts the forward declarations do not carry: element type bf16, the meaning of Rows and Cols, the 64-byte alignment, and the two allowed values of T.

Proposed:
```cpp
// Packed K types (TRON_K_VNNI):
// - k_vnni_tensor holds one bf16 array of [Rows tokens * Cols dimensions]. The
//   array starts at an address that is a multiple of 64 bytes.
// - k_vnni_view refers to that array and computes where each (token, dim) is.
// The view owns no memory. T = bf16 allows writes, and T = const bf16 is
// read-only.
```

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

Replaces lines 8-14 of the file header (lines 1-7 and 15-27 stay). "Under TRON_K_VNNI the K plane of this 128-dim model is the VNNI plane" restates k_vnni::layout_on<head_size> (true for HEAD_SIZE_128 under TRON_K_VNNI, as the gate comment in h/tron/tensor/k_vnni.hpp:91-100 already says), and "(temporary until the VNNI AMX kernel lands, see apply_page_tok)" repeats the callee's own comment at h/tron/models/self_attention.hpp:1881-1884 (Rule 5). The surviving lines carry the facts the test encodes: which gate drops the dense path, what the test then expects, and what "row-major K" marks in the list below.

Proposed:
```cpp
// present. With a VNNI (Vector Neural Network Instructions) K slot
// (k_vnni::layout_on, Note [K VNNI storage] in kv_cache.hpp), apply_page_tok
// compiles out the dense AMX page path. So the Q pack still runs there and no
// kernel runs: the full page takes the AVX path, like a partial page. The
// expectations below say "row-major K" where the two layouts differ.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
proposed lines 1-3 (VNNI K slot, k_vnni::layout_on, apply_page_tok compiles out the dense path) -> line 171 `!k_vnni::layout_on<head_size>` and the `if constexpr` gate at self_attention.hpp:1885-1887; proposed lines 3-4 (Q pack still runs, no kernel runs, full page takes the AVX path) -> lines 194-196 REQUIRE(probe.pack_calls/qk_calls/pv_calls) and the visit_path::avx tallies at lines 206-213; proposed line 5 ("row-major K" marks the layout-dependent expectations) -> lines 15-16 (the host_bf16 full-page expectation that says "for row-major K").

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

Lines 3-7 restate the four TEST_CASE names (lines 101, 120-121, 329-330, 388-389) one by one. Lines 9-11 repeat the both-builds fact that the block at 347-349 states next to the #ifdef it describes (391-395), so they are cut here.

Proposed:
```cpp
// This file tests the VNNI (Vector Neural Network Instructions) K storage layout
// (Note [K VNNI storage] in kv_cache.hpp) through its typed owner and views
// (h/tron/tensor/k_vnni.hpp) and its AVX-512 (the 512-bit Advanced Vector
// Extensions) score reader. No AMX (Advanced Matrix Extensions, the tile
// instruction set) instruction runs here. So every case runs on every host.
```

**[comments] Rule 4, comment lines that map to no code line: shorten**

The salt-range, token-position and 0x0800 sentences restate the constants N_SALT_RANGES_4, SALT_RANGE_0X4000, PATTERN_BASE_0X0800 and the expression on lines 371-372. 'NaN is unequal to itself. That would hide a correct copy.' maps to nothing here: every comparison goes through same_bits (lines 411, 443, 451, 471), which compares bits, so a NaN pattern would not hide a copy. The finiteness fact stays because it is why the base and range values were chosen (checked: the four salt ranges 0x0800..0x27ff, 0x4800..0x67ff, 0x8800..0xa7ff, 0xc800..0xe7ff avoid both inf/NaN ranges).

Proposed:
```cpp
// Distinct finite bf16 bit patterns per (salt, token, dimension). For token < 64
// (one page) no pattern reaches the inf (infinity) / NaN range 0x7f80..0x7fff or
// 0xff80..0xffff.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
line 1 (distinct finite patterns per salt, token, dimension) -> the body, lines 369-373; lines 2-3 (base and salt ranges avoid inf/NaN) -> PATTERN_BASE_0X0800 and SALT_RANGE_0X4000 on lines 367-368 and their use on line 371

**[comments] Rule 4, comment lines that map to no code line: shorten**

The added sentence ("The packed K view below is the distinct type ... the native alias stays for the row-major geometries") maps to none of the three STATIC_REQUIREs under it; it describes the block at 410-442, which introduces itself with "follows the same rules". Cut it and end the block at "does not enforce it."

Proposed:
```cpp
  // Packed views come only from an owner; a bare pointer does not say
  // which layout it points at. The native K alias is the existing row-major
  // view and keeps its pointer constructor: it names the layout at the kernel
  // signature but does not enforce it.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
L1-2 (packed views only from an owner, a bare pointer names no layout) -> 395-396; L2-4 (the native K alias keeps its pointer constructor, names but does not enforce) -> 397.

**[comments] Rule 4, comment lines that map to no code line: shorten**

Two parts map to nothing in this function: "rounded to nearest even on the AMX path" (this test has kv_mul 2, so amx_attn_h128g4::shape_ok is false and no AMX path runs) and the last three sentences, which read the numbers back from the code (`layout_on ? 5e-2 : 2e-2`, WithinAbs 2e-6f, WithinRel 1e-6f). The derivation of 0.02, the two measurements and the reason for a relative s_star bound stay.

Proposed:
```cpp
  // 12. Check that the results for kv head 0 are the same up to a floating-point
  // tolerance. The two runs reach a token's page with a different running
  // maximum m. So each softmax weight exp(s - m) is truncated to bf16 for the
  // PV product (Note [AMX attention dispatch]) at a different scale. One
  // truncation step is up to 2^-7 of the weight. With a 32-token window that
  // moves one element of the weighted V sum by up to about 0.02 (measured
  // 0.018 with row-major K and 0.022 with VNNI K, whose scores differ in the
  // last bits). s_star adds the fp32 weights and differs by a few ulp only.
  // The VNNI bound on it is relative: a few ulp at any magnitude.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
L1-2 (kv head 0 equal up to a tolerance) -> check_kv_head_0_against_saved (2733); L2-3 (two runs, different running maximum m) -> the step 7 vs step 11 apply_page_range runs, the reason a tolerance exists; L3-4 (weight truncated to bf16 at a different scale) -> v_star_tolerance (2732); L4-5 (one truncation step 2^-7) -> the 2e-2 derivation; L5-8 (32-token window, measured 0.018 / 0.022) -> `? 5e-2 : 2e-2` (2732); L8 (s_star few ulp) -> WithinAbs 2e-6f (2741); L9 (relative VNNI bound) -> WithinRel 1e-6f (2738).

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

"This case instantiates apply_page_tok ... (query 0 is hardware-visible and not software-required, so the call scores nothing). It is a compile check first." restates the test name ("compiles for 32 query heads on one 128-dimension K/V head") and the inline comment at 3756-3758, which explains the mask where it is built. The requirement, why this shape, and where the numbers are checked stay.

Proposed:
```cpp
// Software attention must stay available for every accepted geometry, also
// with TRON_K_VNNI. A 128-dimension K/V head shared by 32 query heads is such
// a geometry: the AMX kernel does not take it (its group is 4 heads), and the
// VNNI reader scores it in passes of 16 heads (k_vnni::qk_group). The
// reader's numbers for wide groups are checked in t_k_vnni_layout.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
L1-2 (software attention also with TRON_K_VNNI) -> the [k_vnni] tag and the two builds the case runs in; L2-4 (32 heads on one 128-dimension head: AMX group is 4, qk_group splits at 16) -> head_size / n_heads / n_kv_heads (3704-3706) and the static_assert (3709); L4-5 (numbers checked in t_k_vnni_layout) -> the REQUIREs at 3772-3773 check counts only.

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

"Every stored row must equal its source row bit for bit. Every other row of the book must be untouched." restates the test name ("stores every token's K row once and touches nothing else"); the first sentence describes the callee, which the name and the Note already do; the shape list is repeated run by run in the comments at 3840-3859. The completeness claim and the TRON_K_VNNI-off fact are the lines the code does not say.

Proposed:
```cpp
// The token runs below cover every shape a pass can present to save_k (item 1
// of Note [K VNNI storage] in kv_cache.hpp). With TRON_K_VNNI off the same
// test runs the row-major store.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
L1-2 (every shape a pass can present) -> the add() runs at 3842-3859; L2-3 (TRON_K_VNNI off runs the row-major store) -> the [k_vnni] tag and save_k's row-major branch.

**[comments] Rule 4, comment lines that map to no code line: shorten**

Three of the four sentences map to no line of this test: "each head's row is gathered out of a packed plane into the scratch" restates the K lambda at 888-891 (Rule 2), "populate collects every head's pointer before set_position copies the rows" describes populate's own body (src/tron/gof.cpp:212-218, callee context that belongs at its k_scratch declaration if anywhere), and "the staged bytes are compared per token, head and dimension through the K swizzle" restates the REQUIRE loop at 914-923 and its own comment. The one fact this test adds is what it guards: a wrong per-head scratch stride in populate.

Proposed:
```cpp
// The K callbacks return the scratch row that populate supplies, as the
// scheduler's callback does for a VNNI K plane (Note [K VNNI storage] in
// kv_cache.hpp). A wrong per-head scratch stride in populate fails the check.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
L1-L2 (callbacks return populate's scratch, as the scheduler does; Note pointer) -> the K lambda, lines 888-891. L3 (a wrong per-head stride fails the check) -> the REQUIRE loop, lines 914-923.
